window.PULSE_CURRENT = {"asOf":"2026-11-25T04:55:00Z","calendar":[{"date":"2026-11-25","event":"40-year JGB auction printed: ¥300bn, cover 2.63 (September 3.10), highest yield 4.090%; services PPI (Oct) +4.2% y/y; Tokyo 25 Nov session (Nikkei 56,710.43 at the morning close)","why":"Supply test for the super-long end and the services-price input to December; both printed today."},{"date":"2026-11-26","event":"Tamura speech (Yamanashi); leading and coincident indices (Sep); MoF market-participants (primary dealers) meeting on 2027 issuance; US: PCE deflator (Oct; core consensus 3.2%), Q3 GDP second estimate, durable goods","why":"Tamura's end-point (about 2%) and any market-stability reference; the dealer meeting sets the super-long issuance tone after a weak 40-year auction."},{"date":"2026-11-27","event":"Tokyo CPI (Nov, 08:30; consensus core 2.9% from 2.6%, headline 3.1% from 2.8%, core-core 2.7% from 2.6%); liquidity supply auction (5 to 11 years, ¥500bn); MoF investor meeting; US markets closed (Thanksgiving)","why":"The early read for the 18 Dec national CPI; a core print at or above 2.9% reprices December after the slip to about 78%."},{"date":"2026-11-30","event":"2-year JGB auction (¥2.8tn); Ueda speech and press conference (Nagoya); industrial production prelim (Oct); retail sales (Oct); unemployment and jobs ratio (Oct); housing starts (Oct); MoF intervention total (Nov)","why":"Ueda's last scheduled words before the December meeting; the October hard data test the household leg; the intervention total shows whether the MoF acted near 160."},{"date":"2026-12-01","event":"Manufacturing PMI final (Nov; flash 53.7); Q3 MoF corporate survey (capex); 10-year JGB auction","why":"Confirms the manufacturing leg of the regime and the capex input to the GDP second estimate."},{"date":"2026-12-02","event":"Consumer confidence (Nov; October 35.0)","why":"A print under 35 is the Slowdown trigger on the household leg."},{"date":"2026-12-03","event":"Services PMI final (Nov; flash 50.5); 30-year JGB auction","why":"A services final under 50 moves the regime to Slowdown; the 30-year auction follows a weak 40-year."},{"date":"2026-12-08","event":"Q3 GDP second estimate (08:50; first −0.1% annualized); wages and real wages (Oct); household spending (Oct); US FOMC starts (8–9 Dec, 95.8% for 25 bp)","why":"A second negative quarter on the revision is the Slowdown trigger; real wages are the virtuous-cycle test."},{"date":"2026-12-12","event":"Diet session ends; food consumption-tax cut bill due to pass the Upper House by this date","why":"About ¥5tn a year unfunded; the fiscal input to the super-long end."},{"date":"2026-12-15","event":"BoJ Tankan (Dec; September large manufacturers +24); machinery orders (Oct)","why":"The quarterly leading read two days before the BoJ meeting."},{"date":"2026-12-18","event":"BoJ decision (meeting 17–18 Dec; pricing about 78% for 1.50%); national CPI (Nov, 08:30) the same morning","why":"The base case is a hike to 1.50%; the CPI prints hours before the statement."}],"cb":{"label":"Tightening","note":"Held at 1.25% on 30 Oct by 8–1 with Takata dissenting for 1.50%. October core CPI 2.2%, core-core 2.2%, headline 2.3%: at target. October services PPI (25 Nov, 08:50) rose 4.2% y/y from a revised 3.8%, the fastest since March 1990, with ex-international transport +3.2% and accommodation +12.1%, which supports the services-price leg of the December case. Pricing has slipped: MogWatch OIS puts the 18 Dec hike at 78.2% (implied 1.445%, +19.5 bp; 1.609% by March, 1.75% or higher by March 46%) as of 01:21 JST on 25 Nov against 84.7% on 20 Nov; OANDA has 78.7%; Minkabu had about 81% on 22 Nov and the Reuters poll 86% of economists. JGBs closed 24 Nov (Kyodo, Fisco, Trading Economics): 2-year 1.765% (−5.5 bp), 5-year 2.12%, 10-year 2.720% (−6.5 bp, the new 384th issue, 3.1% coupon, a two-week low with 2.695% intraday per Nikkei), 20-year 3.63%, 30-year 3.94%, 40-year 4.12%, December futures 127.60 (+60 sen) in a 127.15 to 127.84 range, with lower crude and the haven bid through the morning doing the work. The ¥300bn 40-year reopening on 25 Nov drew 2.63 cover against 3.10 in September and below the 12-month average (Bloomberg), highest accepted yield 4.090% (4.125% in September), price 94.35: a weak auction at a lower yield, and Trading Economics shows the 30-year 3.96% (+2 bp) and 40-year 4.14% (+2.5 bp) after it. Tamura speaks in Yamanashi on 26 Nov; the Tokyo CPI on 27 Nov has consensus core 2.9% (October 2.6%), headline 3.1%, core-core 2.7%; Ueda speaks in Nagoya on 30 Nov. The yen: USD/JPY closed 159.26 in New York on 24 Nov (OANDA, +0.24; Fisco 159.35) after a 159.58 high in the Tokyo evening and 158.99 low near 22:00 JST, 159.31 to 159.34 at midday Tokyo on 25 Nov; EUR/JPY 174.64, EUR/USD 1.0966, dollar index low 103.75; no MoF or Katayama comment dated 24 or 25 Nov was found. The Fed: CME FedWatch 95.8% for 25 bp on 8–9 Dec and 4.2% for 50 bp (OANDA, 24 Nov evening), with a cumulative 50 bp by January at 65.8%; US 10-year 4.90% to 4.93% (−1 to −2 bp), November consumer confidence 103.3 against 92.4 consensus, Richmond Fed 3. The balance sheet was ¥626.2tn at 31 Oct. Next: Tamura 26 Nov, Tokyo CPI and liquidity supply auction 27 Nov, 2-year auction and Ueda 30 Nov, GDP second estimate 8 Dec, FOMC 8–9 Dec, national CPI and the BoJ decision 18 Dec."},"cbName":"BoJ stance","conflicts":[{"a":"The leading data still say Expansion: the November flash manufacturing PMI rose to 53.7 with export orders at their second-fastest growth since January 2018, October exports were a record ¥11.77tn (+20.5%), core machinery orders +13.4% y/y and the October services PPI printed +4.2% y/y, a 36-year high.","b":"The household leg is turning: services PMI 50.5 from 51.7 with new business at a four-month low, the composite at a six-month low, Q3 GDP −0.1% annualized on consumption −0.3% q/q, consumer confidence 35.0, and the equity drawdown is 22% on the 25 Nov morning level.","read":"Leading over coincident keeps the regime Expansion on the manufacturing leg; the services side has not crossed 50 and the hard October data are not in yet, so the conflict is open.","trigger":"Services PMI under 50 in the final (early December), consumer confidence under 35 on 2 Dec, or a second negative GDP quarter on 8 Dec moves the regime to Slowdown."},{"a":"Breadth says the fall is over: on 25 Nov 866 Prime names rose against 640 and 21 of 33 sectors gained while the Nikkei fell 3.30%; on 24 Nov 26 of 33 sectors rose and TOPIX ended a five-session losing streak.","b":"The index says it is not: three names (Advantest −841 points, Tokyo Electron −593, SoftBank Group −409) took the Nikkei to 56,710.43 at the morning close, 274 points above the 20 Nov liquidation close, after the SOX fell 7.88% and Nvidia 5.86%; the first stabilisation test (60,030 and 3,895) has not been passed.","read":"The drawdown has narrowed to the AI complex, which is a better reading than 20 Nov, but the Nikkei is the index the posture is set on and it can make a new low on those names alone.","trigger":"A close above 60,030 with TOPIX above 3,895 and the Nikkei VI under 35 moves the posture to Neutral; a close under 56,436.65 is a new drawdown low."},{"a":"December is still the base case: 78.2% per MogWatch OIS, 78.7% per OANDA, 86% of economists in the Reuters poll; the October services PPI at +4.2% y/y and core CPI at 2.2% give the Bank its case, and Ueda said on 18 and 19 Nov the Bank will keep tightening.","b":"The pricing has slipped from 84.7% on 20 Nov and 88% before the CPI miss, the 2-year closed 1.765% on 24 Nov, the first close under 1.80% in nine sessions, and the 40-year auction drew 2.63 cover, below the 12-month average, so the market is pricing part of a hold without a BoJ word to justify it.","read":"The drift is the equity drawdown and the weak super-long demand, not the inflation data; the Bank has not spoken since 19 Nov.","trigger":"Tamura on 26 Nov and Ueda on 30 Nov: any reference to market stability is the hold tell; a 2-year close back above 1.85% after the Tokyo CPI on 27 Nov (core consensus 2.9%) reprices December."},{"a":"The yen is off the haven path: USD/JPY closed 159.26 on 24 Nov and was 159.31 to 159.34 at midday on 25 Nov with the Nikkei down 3.3%, EUR/JPY 174.64, and the front-end gap is about 290 bp with the Fed at 95.8% for 25 bp.","b":"The intervention side is 74 sen away: the 159.58 high on 24 Nov was above Monday's 159.50, the week's high is 159.97, no MoF or Katayama comment has been found since 19 Nov, and the November intervention total prints 30 Nov.","read":"An equity fall confined to AI names does not produce a yen bid; the pair is trading the rate gap and the 160 line is the next event.","trigger":"A close above 160 brings the MoF; a return of the broad equity fall (TOPIX under 3,674) takes the pair toward 158.4 and the 155 to 156 carry-unwind zone."},{"a":"The super-long end is holding: the 30-year closed 3.94% on 24 Nov, a third close under 4.00%, and the 40-year auction cleared at 4.090%, under September's 4.125%.","b":"The auction was weak: 2.63 cover against 3.10 and below the 12-month average, and the 30-year was 3.96% and the 40-year 4.14% after it, with the MoF primary-dealer and investor meetings on 26 and 27 Nov and the unfunded food tax cut (about ¥5tn a year) in the Diet.","read":"The lower yield was taken as a concession before the sale and real money did not chase it; the fiscal-stress gauge stays on warn with the yen at 159.","trigger":"A 30-year close above 4.05% while equities fall is the term-premium signal and 4.30% the fiscal line; a close under 3.90% with USD/JPY under 158.5 turns the warn pair off."}],"drivers":[{"date":"2026-11-25","impact":"The rebound did not hold and the drawdown has narrowed to the AI complex; the 20 Nov liquidation close (56,436.65) is 274 points away.","src":"Fisco via Zaikei 879072; OANDA Japan DZH mn_1045705; Media-IR","tag":"Markets","title":"Tokyo 25 Nov morning: Nikkei 56,710.43 (−1,934.20, −3.30%) after opening 58,383.49 (−261.14) and falling more than 2,000 intraday; TOPIX 3,727.24 (−37.64, −1.00%); futures 56,710 (−2,270); Prime 866 up, 640 down, 21 of 33 sectors up (pharmaceuticals, food, other products); Advantest 23,375 (−3,900, −841 points), Tokyo Electron 7,773 (−1,173, −593), SoftBank Group 2,661 (−505, −409), about 95% of the fall; Ibiden −16.5%, Murata −16.1%, Kioxia −15.1%, Fujikura −14.0%; Fast Retailing +121 points; volume 1,268m shares, Prime turnover about ¥3.64tn.","url":"https://www.zaikei.co.jp/article/20261125/879072.html"},{"date":"2026-11-25","impact":"A weak super-long auction at a lower yield: the concession was taken before the sale and real money did not chase it; the fiscal-stress gauge stays on warn ahead of the MoF meetings on 26 and 27 Nov.","src":"Newsquawk; StockNow; Bloomberg headline; Trading Economics","tag":"Markets","title":"40-year JGB auction (¥300bn reopening of the 19th issue, results 12:35): cover 2.63 against 3.10 in September and below the 12-month average (Bloomberg); highest accepted yield 4.090% (4.125% in September) at a price of 94.35. Trading Economics showed the 30-year at 3.96% (+2 bp) and the 40-year at 4.14% (+2.5 bp) after the result; the 24 Nov closes were 3.94% and 4.12%.","url":"https://www.newsquawk.com/headlines/japan-sells-jpy-300bln-in-40-year-jgbs-bc-263x-prev-310x-highest-accepted-yield-4090-prev-4125"},{"date":"2026-11-25","impact":"Services prices are running well above 2% at the corporate level, which supports the BoJ's December case even as the market trims it to about 78%.","src":"Bank of Japan","tag":"Data","title":"October services PPI (BoJ, 08:50): +4.2% y/y from a revised 3.8%, the fastest since March 1990, m/m +1.1%; ex-international transport +3.2% (3.0%); international transport +99.1% (ocean freight +120.4%), transport and postal +9.4%, accommodation +12.1%, leasing and rental +11.8%, real estate +3.0%, information and communications +2.5%, advertising −1.2%.","url":"https://www.boj.or.jp/statistics/pi/cspi_release/sppi2610.pdf"},{"date":"2026-11-24","impact":"Monday's Huang rally unwound in the chips and memory names that lead the Nikkei while the Dow rose; the rotation out of AI is the Tokyo driver on 25 Nov.","src":"Company Chronicle; Motley Fool; TradingKey; OANDA Japan NY digest","tag":"Markets","title":"Wall Street 24 Nov: S&P 500 7,201.09 (−36.08, −0.50%), Nasdaq 23,733.54 (−356.02, −1.48%), Nasdaq 100 −1.99%, Dow 50,018.70 (+423.39, +0.85%), Russell 2000 2,684.94 (+0.57%), VIX 26.36; Philadelphia Semiconductor Index −7.88%; Nvidia $155.38 (−$9.67, −5.86%, more than −8% intraday, 379m shares; Bernstein upgrade to Outperform, $200), AMD $413.27 (−8.86%), Micron −10.97%, SanDisk −11.13%, HPE −16.31%, Broadcom $301.08 (−3.98%), Anthropic $81.91 (−6.5%, under the $83 IPO price, $75.61 low), Microsoft +0.7%, Apple +1.4%; Dell: AI orders $60.2bn, backlog $132.3bn, inventory nearly tripled; US 10-year 4.90% to 4.93%; gold $3,561.9 (−89.5); WTI $82.05 (+1.88).","url":"https://www.fool.com/coverage/stock-market-today/2026/11/24/stock-market-today-nov-24-nvidia-gives-back-usd240-billion-in-market-value-despite-wall-street-upgrade-signaling-29-upside/"},{"date":"2026-11-24","impact":"The report pushed US futures and yields down and USD/JPY to its 158.99 low; for Japan the headline rate matches the existing deal, so the export leg is unchanged until the findings are published.","src":"WSJ via Fisco (Zaikei 878965); OANDA Japan NY digest","tag":"Trade","title":"WSJ (24 Nov, via Fisco): the Trump administration is preparing higher tariffs under Section 301 on imports from 16 economies including China, Japan, Korea, the EU, India and Vietnam, with USTR Greer able to publish the industrial-subsidy findings of the probe opened on 11 March as early as this week; Japan, Korea and the EU would be held to about 15% in total under their deals, China higher, autos, steel and aluminium excluded, with a clause to raise rates on added excess capacity. The Supreme Court struck down the IEEPA tariffs on 20 February; the July forced-labour tariffs (10% or 12.5% on 60 partners) may stack.","url":"https://www.zaikei.co.jp/article/20261125/878965.html"},{"date":"2026-11-24","impact":"The yen weakened on the day the Nikkei rose and stayed weak as it fell, with the front-end gap about 290 bp; December BoJ pricing is drifting lower without a BoJ word.","src":"OANDA Japan NY digest mn_1045662; Fisco via Zaikei 878985; OANDA Japan FOMC preview; MogWatch","tag":"Policy","title":"US rates and the yen, 24 Nov: USD/JPY closed 159.26 in New York (OANDA, +0.24; Fisco 159.35) after a 159.58 high and 158.99 low; EUR/JPY 174.64 (+1.27), EUR/USD 1.0966 (+0.0063), dollar index low 103.75; US 10-year 4.93% (−1 bp; Company Chronicle 4.90%); November consumer confidence 103.3 (consensus 92.4, October 91.9), Richmond Fed 3 (−2), Case-Shiller +2.6% y/y, FHFA 0.0% m/m; CME FedWatch 95.8% for 25 bp on 8–9 Dec, 4.2% for 50 bp, cumulative 50 bp by January 65.8% (OANDA, 24 Nov evening). BoJ OIS: MogWatch 78.2% for 18 Dec at 01:21 JST on 25 Nov (implied 1.445%), OANDA 78.7%.","url":"https://www.oanda.jp/lab-education/market_news/20261124_fomc_dec_preview/"},{"date":"2026-11-24","impact":"The first post-holiday close confirmed a 3.91% rebound that still failed the 60,030 and 3,895 lines, while bonds rallied with crude lower.","src":"News On Japan; ad-hoc-news/AP; Reuters via WHTC; Kyodo via OANDA Japan; Fisco via Zaikei 878940; Trading Economics","tag":"Markets","title":"Tokyo close 24 Nov: Nikkei 58,644.63 (+2,207.98, +3.91%), high 59,262.57, up more than 2,800 at one point (Jiji), Prime turnover about ¥8.07tn, 26 of 33 sectors up led by nonferrous metals, electrical equipment and glass; Advantest +12.5%, Tokyo Electron +7.7% (AP), Ibiden limit-up, Murata and Taiyo Yuden sharply higher, Baycurrent a record; Inpex, Kobe Bussan, Nippon Shinyaku, Dentsu and KDDI fell. TOPIX 3,764.88 (+90.66, +2.47%), ending five straight falls. JGBs: 2-year 1.765% (−5.5 bp), 5-year 2.12%, 10-year 2.720% (−6.5 bp, 2.695% intraday), 20-year 3.63%, 30-year 3.94%, 40-year 4.12%, December futures 127.60 (+60 sen, 127.15 to 127.84). Reuters poll (22 analysts, 16 to 24 Nov): Nikkei 65,000 by June 2027, 67,500 by end-2027, 69,000 by mid-2028.","url":"https://newsonjapan.com/article/151515.php"},{"date":"2026-11-24","impact":"Manufacturing keeps the regime in Expansion and the services slide is the first survey evidence of the equity fall reaching households; the composite at a six-month low with confidence at a 2020 low is the warn for the household leg.","src":"Reuters via Investing.com; ActionForex; au.investing; IndexBox","tag":"Data","title":"November flash PMIs (S&P Global / au Jibun Bank, 08:30): manufacturing 53.7 from 53.3 (consensus 53.5), output 53.8 from 53.0, new orders solid, manufacturing export orders the second-fastest growth since January 2018 on Asian semiconductor and electronics demand, employment up, outstanding work rising a twelfth month; services 50.5 from 51.7 with new business at a four-month low; composite 51.5 from 52.1, a six-month low; business confidence joint-lowest since August 2020; input costs an eight-month low, output charges the weakest since March. Fiddes: 'momentum was lost amid a slowdown in service sector activity'.","url":"https://www.actionforex.com/live-comments/661847-japan-pmi-growth-slows-to-six-month-low-as-services-weaken-despite-manufacturing-strength/"},{"date":"2026-11-23","impact":"The governance shock that drove the 20 Nov liquidation reversed in one session; the SEC and DOJ inquiries continue (Al Jazeera), so the AI chain's governance strand is open even as the price has recovered.","src":"Nvidia newsroom and SEC 8-K; Al Jazeera; Motley Fool; Fox Business; STL.News; TBL Advisory","tag":"Markets","title":"Nvidia's board 'voted unanimously' to reinstate Jensen Huang as president, CEO and director effective immediately, citing 'input from senior levels of the United States government', 'no national security risk' and satisfaction with his 'candor', with an independent-director investigation of the reported foreign-intelligence effort, a new National Security and Export Control Committee, a chief compliance officer search and continued cooperation with government investigations; Kress continues as CFO. Trump: 'the correct and proper decision', he was 'represented in the board meeting'. Nvidia $165.05 (+$39.52, +31.48%, range $156.93 to $171.50, 772.5m shares, about $3.03tn; largest one-day percentage gain in more than two decades per Motley Fool); S&P 500 7,237.17 (+326.78, +4.73%), Nasdaq 24,089.56 (+1,471.82, +6.51%), Dow 49,595.31 (+1,447.98, +3.01%), Russell 2000 2,669.66 (+3.40%), VIX 26.62 from 35.89; Micron +19%, AMD $453.46 (+6.85%), Broadcom $313.55 (+3.00%), Oracle +12.34%, Tesla +11.5%, Meta +6.6%, Anthropic $87.64 (−1.4%). Chicago Nikkei futures 57,960.","url":"https://nvidianews.nvidia.com/news/nvidia-board-reinstates-jensen-huang-as-president-ceo-and-board-director"},{"date":"2026-11-23","impact":"The yen gave back 45 sen of its haven bid as equities rebounded; the rate gaps (about 286 bp at the front end) hold USD/JPY near 159 with 160 and the MoF 98 sen away, and December is still the base case on both sides.","src":"Reuters via Kitco; NY Fed Teller Window; Fox Business; Trading Economics; Fisco via Zaikei 878815; OANDA Japan NY digest; TBL Advisory; Minkabu FX","tag":"Policy","title":"US rates and the yen, 23 Nov: Williams (Bronx) said inflation is 'too high' and 'there is still work to do', has no particular timing in mind and 'would not exclude' moving at consecutive meetings, with PCE inflation about 3.5% this year, just above 2% next year and 2% in 2028, GDP growth about 2.75%; CME FedWatch 96% for 25 bp on 8–9 Dec (Fox Business), Trading Economics about 93% with 50 bp largely ruled out, Bessent warning against excessive tightening. The $69bn 2-year auction stopped at 4.703% with 2.653 cover (six-auction average 2.63), a −0.3 bp tail and 51.5% indirects; the 10-year fell from 4.98% to 4.94% (flat on the day), the 2-year 4.683% (+3.6 bp). USD/JPY closed 159.02 in New York (+0.45) after a 159.50 high and a 158.87 low; EUR/JPY 173.37, lowest since October 2025; EUR/USD 1.0903 after 1.0881, lowest since April 2025; dollar index high 104.53, highest since March 2025; WTI $80.17 (−3.24), gold $3,651.4 (+136.0). Minkabu's weekly preview (22 Nov): December BoJ pricing about 81% after the CPI miss from about 88%; two hikes by March 47% from 54%.","url":"https://www.oanda.jp/lab-education/market_news/mn_1045448_202611240720/"},{"date":"2026-11-20","impact":"The liquidation read is confirmed on the index leg (close under 57,435 on 93% negative breadth and the heaviest turnover since 6 Nov) and not on the volatility leg (VI under 40); the first stabilisation test is 6% away on both index legs. Tokyo reopens 24 Nov with the flash PMIs and futures at 54,400.","src":"Fisco via Zaikei 878619; TBL Advisory 20261120nk; OANDA Japan DZH mn_1045088 and the 20261120 explainer; stock-marketdata.com (VI); OANDA Japan NY digest (night futures)","tag":"Markets","title":"Tokyo close on 20 Nov: Nikkei 56,436.65 (−3,007.56, −5.06%), the fifth-largest point fall on record (after −4,451.28 on 5 Aug 2024, −3,836.48 on 20 Oct 1987, −3,643.13 on 6 Nov 2026 and −3,318 on 15 Oct 2026), the first close in the 56,000s in about seven months, 998 points under the 57,435 liquidation line and 22.0% below the 72,366.34 record; open 58,551.56, high 58,707.48, down more than 2,800 by 9:50, −2,198 at 10:25, 56,775.04 at the morning close, past −3,000 in the 14:40s, closed on the low at 15:30; week −7.7% from 61,142.35. TOPIX 3,674.22 (−148.25, −3.88%), a fifth fall; Growth 250 638.22 (−5.45%). Prime 90 up / 1,443 down (93%), turnover ¥8,638.9bn on 3,085.63m shares; 2 of 33 sectors up (insurance +0.65%, air transport +0.33%), nonferrous metals −9.90%, electrical equipment −5.70%, glass and ceramics −5.21%, machinery −4.99%, pharmaceuticals −0.58%. Advantest (−10.3%) and SoftBank Group (−14.5%) about 1,016 index points, top five about 1,527; Fujikura −12.2%, Kioxia −11.7%, Lasertec −9.2%, Disco −8.3%, Tokyo Electron −7.1%, TDK −6.9%, Mitsubishi Heavy −5.5%, Shin-Etsu −5.3%, Hitachi −4.7%, Toyota −2.0%, Fast Retailing −1.4%, Sony −1.2%; Tokio Marine +2.0%, Otsuka second-largest positive contributor. Nikkei VI 33.07 (+2.43). December futures 56,420 (−3,320); night session opened 56,760 and fell to 54,400 after Wall Street. USD/JPY 159.20 at 16:00 Tokyo. Fisco and OANDA cite Anthropic sell-the-news, the AMD short report, Fed hike pricing and the three-day weekend; Osaka holiday futures trading on 23 Nov is cancelled.","url":"https://www.oanda.jp/lab-education/market_news/mn_1045088_202611201757/"},{"date":"2026-11-20","impact":"Bull-flattening from the belly with the front end finally giving ground: December stays priced (eighth close above 1.80%) but the market has started to charge for the chance the Bank waits. The super-long end rallied least ahead of the 25 Nov 40-year sale.","src":"QUICK/NQN via SMTB; Fisco via Zaikei 878620; stock-marketdata.com; MoF auction result resul20261120; jp-bond.com","tag":"Markets","title":"JGB closes 20 Nov (QUICK/NQN via SMTB, 15:29): 2-year 1.820% (−2.5 bp; Fisco 1.816%), 5-year 2.180% (−5.5 bp), 10-year 2.785% (−3.5 bp, fifth fall; stock-marketdata.com 2.785%, Fisco 2.767%), 20-year 3.685% (−3.5 bp), 30-year 3.975% (−1.5 bp), 40-year 4.150% (−3 bp); December futures 127.00 (+35 sen; range 126.70 to 127.04). The 465th liquidity supply auction (1 to 5 years, issue 24 Nov): ¥2,556.8bn bid for ¥699.1bn accepted, cover 3.66 against 4.42, average spread −0.020%, maximum −0.017% (72.5% allotted), called slightly weak; the 3-month bill drew 1.2742% (1.2800%) on 3.67 cover (3.90). QUICK cites haven buying as US stocks fell on 19 Nov and the Nikkei lost more than 3,000 yen, with US yields falling during Tokyo hours (2-year about 4.73%, 10-year about 5.01% at 15:36 JST per Fisco). MoF reference rates for 19 Nov (jp-bond.com, compound basis): 2-year 1.853%, 10-year 2.870%, 30-year 4.030%, 40-year 4.092%.","url":"https://www.mof.go.jp/jgbs/auction/calendar/nyusatsu/resul20261120.htm"},{"date":"2026-11-20","impact":"The AI complex is being repriced on governance and valuation at once; the US indices joined Tokyo in correction and the Nikkei night futures fell to 54,400. The SOX close was not found.","src":"AP via Yakima Herald (index table); CappNotes closing look; BigGo Finance (SEC probe, Nvidia week); Motley Fool (Nvidia, AMD, Broadcom)","tag":"Markets","title":"Wall Street 20 Nov: S&P 500 6,910.39 (−265.44, −3.70%), a seventh straight loss, more than 10% off its 6 Oct record and in a correction, −8.6% on the week, +0.9% for 2026; Nasdaq 22,617.73 (−1,261.47, −5.28%), −10% on the week, −2.7% for the year; Nasdaq-100 25,028.98 (−5.61%); Dow 48,147.33 (−949.73, −1.93%), about 10% off its record, lowest since 10 Apr, −8.0% on the week; Russell 2000 2,581.79 (−2.76%, −7.5% w/w); VIX 35.89 (+26.2%), highest close since late March. Nvidia $125.53 (−$20.45, −14.01%; range $124.50 to $143.73, a 52-week low; 607.8m shares, about 262% of average; worst day since January 2025; −23% and about $900bn on the week, the largest single-week market-cap loss on record; about $2.5tn off the peak) after Reuters reported the SEC is investigating its accounting: Nvidia moved its regional revenue disclosure from billing address to customer headquarters and stopped showing Singapore (22% of revenue as of July 2025, home to Megaspeed, on the sales ban list since June 2025) separately; the SEC declined comment, Nvidia did not respond, timing and focus unconfirmed; California's attorney general subpoenaed Nvidia on 19 Nov; Huang testifies to the House on 1 Dec and the Senate on 3 Dec. AMD $424.32 (−13.25%), Broadcom $304.65 (−12.19%), Micron −13.89%; Anthropic $88.91 (−8.50%) after touching about its $83 IPO price, 7.1% above it at the close. Kospi 4,869.96 (−5.21%), Taiex 36,033.29 (−2,393.60, −6.23%; −17.5% w/w), Hang Seng −1.89%; bitcoin about $58,994 (−4.7%).","url":"https://finance.biggo.com/news/d18e569f-526d-447e-980b-10bb46a6fe95"},{"date":"2026-11-20","impact":"The carry-unwind side of the yen framework is live: equities down, US yields down 12 to 13 bp, yen up 1.12, gaps narrowed to about 281 bp and 214 bp. 160 is 1.4 yen away and the intervention question is parked while the fall lasts.","src":"CappNotes closing look; Fisco via Zaikei 878678 (US bonds) and 878669 (New York FX); OANDA Japan NY digest mn_1045222; Kyodo via Ryukyu Shimpo","tag":"Policy","title":"US rates and the yen, 20 Nov: 2-year 4.63% (−13 bp; Fisco 4.65%), 10-year 4.92% (−12 bp; Fisco 4.94%, OANDA 4.95%), 30-year 5.31% (Fisco 5.34%), the curve steepening as the front end rallied; dollar index −0.07% to about 103.84 from Thursday's 19-month high. USD/JPY closed 158.57 in New York (OANDA; −1.12), the yen's largest gain since 6 Nov, after 159.20 at 16:00 Tokyo and a 158.92 to 158.42 New York range (Fisco; low near 05:00 JST); EUR/JPY 173.92 after a 173.74 low, a 13-month low; EUR/USD 1.0968. No MoF or Katayama comment dated 20 Nov found. Kalshi: 90% for a 25 bp Fed hike on 9 Dec, 9% hold, 2% larger; Cook (in Chile) said waiting longer to raise rates risks falling behind the curve and that she had preferred a hike in October; Waller's 20 Nov remarks not found. Michigan final November sentiment 51.5 (prelim 51.0, October 48.0; forecast 51.6), one-year inflation expectations 3.7% (4.3% prelim), the lowest since March. WTI settled $83.98 (+0.93%), a third weekly fall; Brent $92.57 (+0.64%); gold $3,515 (−4.63%), a 14.5-month low and the lowest settlement since August 2025; silver −7.41%.","url":"https://www.oanda.jp/lab-education/market_news/mn_1045222_202611210715/"},{"date":"2026-11-20","impact":"December is not the question; the 18 Dec press conference is. A vague end-point with a hike is the 'hike and sell' yen risk; a clear one into a 22% equity drawdown is the market-stability risk Shinke named.","src":"Reuters via Investing.com (analysis 5040426); InvestingLive; CappNotes (Barchart); Minkabu FX weekly","tag":"Policy","title":"Reuters analysis (Kihara, 20 Nov): BoJ policymakers mostly agree on a December hike to 1.50%, but the split over the end-point could tempt yen bears. Staff put the neutral range at 1.1% to 2.5%; Tamura sees about 2%, Takata wants a level near the middle, Asada and Sato see limited room, Koeda says neutral is 'likely significantly above 1%' and Masu is a swing vote; Tamura's and Takata's terms expire in July, giving Takaichi two appointments. Ueda told parliament on 19 Nov that soft consumption was temporary and gave little on the neutral rate. Iwashita (Nomura): the Bank 'may be hoping to avoid giving too clear a guidance on the pace of rate hikes' and sustained US hikes keep pressure on it to follow; Inadome (SuMi Trust AM): 'yen moves will be key to how far the BOJ will raise rates', 2.0% next year or 2.25% around summer if the yen keeps weakening. The December 2025 precedent: the yen fell on a hike judged unconvincing. InvestingLive adds that intervention risk near 160 caps USD/JPY more than rate expectations do and the July board changes add a dovish tail. Barchart's December hike probability was 79% on 20 Nov (CappNotes) against about 85% on 16 Nov (Minkabu).","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy-news/analysisbojs-ambiguity-on-rate-hike-path-could-tempt-yen-bears-5040426"},{"date":"2026-11-20","impact":"At target and a touch under forecast: December stays priced (2-year 1.83% on the day) and the overshoot case is trimmed; the only caveat in the coverage is market instability, which the same morning's 4.5% equity fall makes the live one.","src":"Reuters via Investing.com and AOL; Trading Economics; ChainCatcher (headline); Fisco via Zaikei (QUICK median)","tag":"Data","title":"October national CPI (MIC, 08:30): core (ex fresh food) +2.2% y/y against a 2.3% consensus (Reuters poll of 16; QUICK median 2.3%) and 2.1% in September, the highest since December 2025; core-core (ex fresh food and energy) +2.2% from 2.4%, a six-month high; headline +2.3% from 2.1%; food +2.2% from 2.9%, goods +3.2% from 2.8%, services +1.4%. Minami (Norinchukin): 'strong chance' of a December hike and another in March 2027 'when inflation is likely to exceed 3%'; Shinke (Dai-ichi Life): 'if markets remain unstable, the BOJ may prefer to stand pat next month'; economy minister Kiuchi: 'signs the economy is moving closer toward a desirable state'.","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economic-indicators/japan-oct-core-cpi-rises-22-yryr-5040343"}],"headline":"Tokyo closed 24 Nov at 58,644.63 (+2,207.98, +3.91%) and TOPIX 3,764.88 (+2.47%), then gave most of it back on 25 Nov: the Nikkei was 56,710.43 (−1,934.20, −3.30%) at the morning close with Advantest, Tokyo Electron and SoftBank Group about 95% of the fall, after the Philadelphia Semiconductor Index fell 7.88% and Nvidia 5.86% on 24 Nov (S&P 500 −0.50%, Nasdaq −1.48%, Dow +0.85%). The ¥300bn 40-year auction drew 2.63 cover against 3.10 in September, below the 12-month average, at a 4.090% highest yield; JGBs closed 24 Nov at 2-year 1.765%, 10-year 2.720% (−6.5 bp), 30-year 3.94%; October services PPI rose 4.2% y/y, the fastest since 1990; USD/JPY closed 159.26 in New York; December BoJ pricing is about 78%.","marketClose":"Tue 24 Nov 2026","notes":["This refresh ran at about 04:55 UTC (13:55 JST) on Wednesday 25 Nov, during the Tokyo afternoon session, so the Nikkei, TOPIX and JGB rows and the history hold the 24 Nov closes (marketClose) and the 25 Nov morning-session levels and the 40-year auction result are in the text only.","Nikkei 24 Nov close 58,644.63 (+2,207.98, +3.91%) is News On Japan, ad-hoc-news (AP) and Reuters via WHTC, which agree; a jkn.co.kr item dated 24 Nov giving 58,576.50 (−283.93) does not match any other source and was not used. TOPIX 3,764.88 (+90.66) is News On Japan only; no second source for the TOPIX close was found. Nikkei VI for 24 Nov was not found (the row text keeps the 20 Nov 33.07).","JGBs 24 Nov: the 10-year 2.720% (−6.5 bp) is Kyodo via OANDA Japan and stock-marketdata.com; Fisco prints 2.696% on an unstated basis. The 2-year 1.765% and 30-year 3.94% are Trading Economics (Fisco 1.766% for the 2-year; no QUICK/NQN or SMTB close was found), so the 2-year and 30-year changes (−5.5 bp, −3.5 bp) are against the 20 Nov QUICK/NQN closes and carry rounding of up to 1 bp. The 5-year (2.12%), 20-year (3.63%) and 40-year (4.12%) are Trading Economics; Fisco has 2.100% and 3.605%. The 25 Nov post-auction 30-year 3.96% and 40-year 4.14% are Trading Economics intraday readings.","40-year auction (25 Nov): ¥300bn, cover 2.63 (September 3.10), highest accepted yield 4.090% (September 4.125%), price 94.35 are Newsquawk and StockNow; \"weaker demand than the 12-month average\" is the Bloomberg headline only. The average yield and tail were not found; the MoF results page had not been linked when checked.","USD/JPY 159.26 for 24 Nov is OANDA Japan's New York digest close (+0.24 from 159.02); Fisco via Zaikei 878985 gives 159.35 at the close and EUR/USD 1.0980 against OANDA's 1.0966. Wall Street 24 Nov closes are the Company Chronicle (Yahoo data at 4:37pm ET), Motley Fool and TradingKey, which agree on the index levels; WTI is $82.05 (OANDA, TradingKey) against $81.53 (Company Chronicle, contract roll).","Services PPI is the BoJ release of 25 Nov (sppi2610.pdf); \"fastest since March 1990\" is Trading Economics' text. BoJ December pricing: MogWatch 78.2% is as of 01:21 JST on 25 Nov, OANDA 78.7% as of the 24 Nov evening; both are OIS-derived and differ from the Minkabu (about 81%, 22 Nov) and Barchart (79%, 20 Nov) figures in the earlier text. Tokyo CPI consensus is Fisco via Zaikei and Minkabu. Calendar and next-release dates are Tokyo dates."],"posture":{"label":"Risk-off","note":"Risk-off. The 24 Nov close is confirmed: Nikkei 58,644.63 (+2,207.98, +3.91%; high 59,262.57, up more than 2,800 intraday per Jiji), TOPIX 3,764.88 (+90.66, +2.47%), Prime turnover about ¥8.07tn, 26 of 33 sectors up, led by nonferrous metals, electrical equipment and glass; Advantest +12.5%, Tokyo Electron +7.7% (AP). That close failed the first stabilisation test (60,030 and 3,895) by 2.3% and 3.3%. Wall Street then gave part of Monday back on 24 Nov: S&P 500 7,201.09 (−0.50%), Nasdaq 23,733.54 (−1.48%), Dow 50,018.70 (+0.85%), Russell 2000 2,684.94 (+0.57%), VIX 26.36 (from 26.62), the Philadelphia Semiconductor Index −7.88%, Nvidia $155.38 (−5.86%, down more than 8% intraday), Micron −10.97%, AMD −8.86%, Broadcom −3.98%, Anthropic $81.91 under its $83 IPO price, with the WSJ tariff report and a Dell inventory warning adding to the AI-capex doubt. Tokyo on 25 Nov (afternoon session open at this refresh): opened 58,383.49 (−261.14), fell more than 2,000 intraday, morning close 56,710.43 (−1,934.20, −3.30%), December futures 56,710 (−2,270); TOPIX 3,727.24 (−37.64, −1.00%); Prime 866 up against 640 down and 21 of 33 sectors up (pharmaceuticals, food, other products), so the fall is three names: Advantest 23,375 (−3,900, −841 points), Tokyo Electron 7,773 (−1,173, −593) and SoftBank Group 2,661 (−505, −409) are about 95% of the drop, with Ibiden −16.5%, Murata −16.1%, Kioxia −15.1%, Fujikura −14.0%, Lasertec −12.0% and Disco −9.9%; Fast Retailing +120 points. Under the framework the liquidation close of 20 Nov (56,436.65) is 274 points under the morning level, the 57,435 line is back below the index, and the Nikkei 225 is now a two-way market between the AI complex and everything else. The posture stays Risk-off until a close retakes 60,030 and 3,895 with the Nikkei VI under 35; a close under 56,436.65 is a new low for the drawdown."},"regime":{"label":"Expansion","note":"No new growth data since the November flash PMIs (24 Nov): manufacturing 53.7 from 53.3 (consensus 53.5), output 53.8, export orders the second-fastest growth since January 2018; services 50.5 from 51.7 with new business at a four-month low; composite 51.5, a six-month low. The leading set is still in expansion on the manufacturing leg: BoJ Tankan +24, Reuters Tankan +27 and +35, September core machinery orders +13.4% y/y with a +4.3% q/q Q4 plan, consumer confidence 35.0, housing starts −2.7%. Coincident: October exports +20.5% y/y to a record ¥11.77tn, Q3 GDP −0.1% annualized on consumption −0.3% q/q, industrial production +1.7%, real wages +2.2%, unemployment 2.4%, jobs ratio 1.19, retail +2.3%. Prices: October core CPI 2.2%, at target; October services PPI +4.2% y/y (25 Nov), the highest since March 1990, with international transport +99.1% and accommodation +12.1%. The WSJ report (24 Nov) that the US is preparing Section 301 tariffs on 16 economies including Japan, with Japan, Korea and the EU capped near 15% under their deals and autos excluded, is a watch item for the export leg rather than a change to it. The framework reads leading over coincident; manufacturing is at the top of its five-year range and the regime stays Expansion with the household side the weak leg and the services PMI 0.5 point from the warn band. A services PMI under 50 in the final (early December) or the December flash, consumer confidence under 35 on 2 Dec, or a second negative GDP quarter on 8 Dec moves it to Slowdown."},"schema":1,"sections":[{"id":"leading","rows":[{"change":"+0.4","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":50},{"l":"Jan 26","v":51.5},{"l":"Feb 26","v":53},{"l":"Mar 26","v":51.6},{"l":"Apr 26","v":55.1},{"l":"May 26","v":54.5},{"l":"Jun 26","v":54.8},{"l":"Jul 26","v":54.5},{"l":"Aug 26","v":54.9},{"l":"Sep 26","v":54.1},{"l":"Oct 26","v":53.3},{"l":"Nov 26","v":53.7}],"latest":"53.7","name":"au Jibun Bank Manufacturing PMI","next":"early Dec (Nov final)","period":"Nov 2026 (flash)","prior":"53.3","read":"The November flash rose to 53.7 from 53.3 in October (consensus 53.5), the first rise since the 54.9 of August. Output 53.8 from 53.0, new orders grew at a solid pace and export orders had their second-fastest growth since January 2018 on semiconductor and electronics demand from other Asian economies; employment rose, led by manufacturing, and outstanding work grew for a twelfth month. Input cost inflation slowed to an eight-month low and output charges to the weakest since March. Business confidence across both sectors fell to its joint-lowest since August 2020, so the order books are strong and the mood is not. Well above 50 and at the top of the five-year range: good. The final prints in early December.","ref":50,"refLabel":"50 = expansion line","signal":"good","signalWord":"Expansion, chip-led","src":"S&P Global flash via Reuters (Investing.com), ActionForex, au.investing and IndexBox (24 Nov)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Up; beat 53.5","url":"https://www.actionforex.com/live-comments/661847-japan-pmi-growth-slows-to-six-month-low-as-services-weaken-despite-manufacturing-strength/"},{"change":"−1.2","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":51.6},{"l":"Jan 26","v":53.7},{"l":"Feb 26","v":53.8},{"l":"Mar 26","v":53.4},{"l":"Apr 26","v":51},{"l":"May 26","v":50},{"l":"Jun 26","v":52.2},{"l":"Jul 26","v":51.2},{"l":"Aug 26","v":52.5},{"l":"Sep 26","v":51.3},{"l":"Oct 26","v":51.7},{"l":"Nov 26","v":50.5}],"latest":"50.5","name":"au Jibun Bank Services PMI","next":"early Dec (Nov final)","period":"Nov 2026 (flash)","prior":"51.7","read":"The November flash fell to 50.5 from 51.7, the lowest since the 50.0 of May, with new business growth at a four-month low; the composite fell to 51.5 from 52.1, a six-month low, and activity still expanded for a twentieth month. Business confidence is at its joint-lowest since August 2020, with manufacturers more optimistic than services firms; S&P Global's Fiddes said 'momentum was lost amid a slowdown in service sector activity' and that 'uncertainty remains elevated'. This is the first survey taken through the equity fall and the first sign of it in the household-facing data. Still above 50 so the signal stays good by the 50 line, but it is 0.5 point from the warn band and the divergence with manufacturing (53.7) is the widest since the spring; the final prints in early December.","ref":50,"refLabel":"50 = expansion line","signal":"good","signalWord":"Just above 50; composite six-month low","src":"S&P Global flash via Reuters (Investing.com), ActionForex and au.investing (24 Nov)","trend":"down","trendWord":"New business four-month low","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economic-indicators/japans-factory-activity-expands-in-november-pmi-shows-5046775"},{"change":"+2","hist":[{"l":"Dec 23","v":12},{"l":"Mar 24","v":11},{"l":"Jun 24","v":13},{"l":"Sep 24","v":13},{"l":"Dec 24","v":14},{"l":"Mar 25","v":12},{"l":"Jun 25","v":13},{"l":"Sep 25","v":14},{"l":"Dec 25","v":15},{"l":"Mar 26","v":17},{"l":"Jun 26","v":22},{"l":"Sep 26","v":24}],"latest":"+24","name":"Tankan Large Manufacturers DI","next":"mid-Dec (Dec survey)","period":"Sep 2026 survey","prior":"+22","read":"Released 1 Oct. Large manufacturers improved for a sixth quarter to +24, the best since March 2018, while large non-manufacturers slipped to +35 from +37. The December outlook DI is +21 for manufacturers and +30 for non-manufacturers, so firms expect some cooling. FY2026 capex plans for large firms are +11.3%. The monthly Reuters Tankan (11 Nov, survey 27 Oct to 6 Nov) points the other way for December: manufacturers +27 from +22 (consensus +23), the highest since August 2021, non-manufacturers +35 from +23, the highest since June 2018, with electronics +39 on memory-chip demand and the February outlook +28 and +29; respondents cited rising prices and interest rates as the drag. The December BoJ Tankan is due mid-December.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = favorable equals unfavorable","signal":"good","signalWord":"Highest since 2018","src":"Bank of Japan; Reuters Tankan via investingLive and RTTNews","trend":"up","trendWord":"Improving","url":"https://www.boj.or.jp/en/statistics/tk/yoshi/tk2609.htm"},{"change":"−0.4","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":37.5},{"l":"Dec 25","v":37.2},{"l":"Jan 26","v":37.9},{"l":"Feb 26","v":40},{"l":"Mar 26","v":33.3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":32.2},{"l":"May 26","v":33.6},{"l":"Jun 26","v":33.8},{"l":"Jul 26","v":34.9},{"l":"Aug 26","v":35.5},{"l":"Sep 26","v":35.4},{"l":"Oct 26","v":35}],"latest":"35.0","name":"Consumer Confidence Index","next":"2 Dec (Nov)","period":"Oct 2026","prior":"35.4","read":"October fell to 35.0 from 35.4, a touch under the 35.1 consensus and the lowest since July, with every component lower: livelihood 33.2 (33.8), willingness to buy durables 26.8 (27.3), income growth 40.4 (40.5), employment 39.4 (40.0). The index has been in the 32 to 36 band since the March drop from 40.0 when the Hormuz closure lifted fuel prices. The October Eco Watchers survey (10 Nov) was also soft: the current index fell to 46.1 from 47.0 (consensus 46.8), households 45.5 (−0.9), corporate 47.6 (+0.5), and the outlook index fell to 46.5 from 47.4. Energy prices turned positive again in the Tokyo CPI in October, which explains the slip; the November reading prints on 2 Dec.","ref":50,"refLabel":"50 = neutral line","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Below 40 band","src":"Cabinet Office via Trading Economics; Eco Watchers via RTTNews, FX.co and Trading Economics","trend":"down","trendWord":"Lowest since July","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/japan/consumer-confidence"},{"change":"−5.2 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":12.5},{"l":"Nov 25","v":-6.4},{"l":"Dec 25","v":16.8},{"l":"Jan 26","v":13.7},{"l":"Feb 26","v":24.7},{"l":"Mar 26","v":5.9},{"l":"Apr 26","v":15.6},{"l":"May 26","v":-1.9},{"l":"Jun 26","v":16.9},{"l":"Jul 26","v":11.2},{"l":"Aug 26","v":18.6},{"l":"Sep 26","v":13.4}],"latest":"+13.4%","name":"Core Machinery Orders, y/y","next":"mid-Dec (Oct)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"+18.6%","read":"Released 19 Nov. Core orders (private, ex ships and electric power) fell 0.3% on the month to ¥1,066.2bn after +5.2% in August (Newsquawk consensus −0.5%, RTTNews +1.3%), the first fall in two months, and rose 13.4% from a year earlier against +13.7% expected and +18.6% in August. Manufacturers' orders rose 3.9% m/m to ¥547.6bn and non-manufacturers' ex utilities 1.1% to ¥547.2bn; government orders fell 1.3%, overseas orders rose 6.2%, total orders were flat. The Cabinet Office kept its 'signs of a pickup' assessment and the Q4 (Oct to Dec) forecast is +4.3% q/q for core orders, +2.6% for manufacturers, +5.2% for non-manufacturers and +8.8% for total orders. The series is noisy; the Tankan capex plans (+11.3% for large firms) and October machine tool orders (+42.5% y/y, the sixteenth straight gain) are the cleaner reads and point the same way. A double-digit y/y gain with a positive Q4 plan keeps the capex lead in place; the heat has come off the August beat.","signal":"good","signalWord":"Small miss; Q4 plan +4.3% q/q","src":"Cabinet Office via FX.co, RTTNews, Newsquawk, Kyodo (OANDA Japan) and Japan Planners (Q4 forecast)","trend":"down","trendWord":"Slower, still double-digit","url":"https://www.fx.co/en/forex-news/3299479"},{"change":"−8.8 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":3.2},{"l":"Nov 25","v":-8.5},{"l":"Dec 25","v":-1.3},{"l":"Jan 26","v":-0.4},{"l":"Feb 26","v":-4.9},{"l":"Mar 26","v":-29.3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":11.4},{"l":"May 26","v":33.9},{"l":"Jun 26","v":18.6},{"l":"Jul 26","v":8.2},{"l":"Aug 26","v":6.1},{"l":"Sep 26","v":-2.7}],"latest":"−2.7%","name":"Housing Starts, y/y","next":"late Nov (Oct)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"+6.1%","read":"Housing starts fell 2.7% y/y in September against a +2.9% consensus, the first fall since March after five positive months (+33.9% in May to +6.1% in August). Built-for-sale −4.5%, owned −3.4%, rented −1.6%, issued +18.4%. The comparison month, September 2025, was itself −7.3%, so the level is weak rather than flattered by the base. With the 10-year JGB near 2.8% and mortgage rates rising after three BoJ hikes, the housing leg of the leading set has turned down.","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Falls on a weak base, missed","src":"MLIT via RTTNews and Newsquawk","trend":"down","trendWord":"Turns negative","url":"https://www.rttnews.com/3706092/japan-housing-starts-fall-2-7-in-september.aspx"}],"sub":"Forward-looking. Read these first.","title":"Leading indicators"},{"id":"coincident","rows":[{"change":"−1.0 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":1.6},{"l":"Nov 25","v":-2.2},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.6},{"l":"Jan 26","v":0.7},{"l":"Feb 26","v":0.4},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Apr 26","v":2},{"l":"May 26","v":-2.1},{"l":"Jun 26","v":4.9},{"l":"Jul 26","v":3.9},{"l":"Aug 26","v":2.7},{"l":"Sep 26","v":1.7}],"latest":"+1.7%","name":"Industrial Production, y/y","next":"30 Nov (Oct prelim)","period":"Sep 2026 (final)","prior":"+2.7%","read":"The September final (METI, 16 Nov) revised output to +2.0% m/m from the +2.2% preliminary and to +1.7% y/y from +1.9% (consensus +1.9%), after −2.4% m/m in August; a ninth positive y/y month, slower on the base. The preliminary detail stands: eight of fifteen sectors rose, led by motor vehicles, production machinery and general-purpose and business-oriented machinery, with iron and steel down; shipments +0.6%, inventories +2.5%. METI's survey projects +5.1% in October and +0.8% in November, so Q4 starts with output catching up to the order book even as Q3 GDP fell 0.1% annualized on consumption and imports. October preliminary is due 30 Nov.","signal":"good","signalWord":"m/m rebound held at +2.0%","src":"METI final via Newsquawk and FX.co (16 Nov); preliminary via investingLive and RTTNews","trend":"down","trendWord":"Revised down","url":"https://www.newsquawk.com/headlines/japanese-industrial-production-final-sep-yy-17-prev-27"},{"change":"−0.6 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":1.7},{"l":"Nov 25","v":1},{"l":"Dec 25","v":-0.9},{"l":"Jan 26","v":1.8},{"l":"Feb 26","v":-0.2},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1.7},{"l":"Apr 26","v":2.1},{"l":"May 26","v":5.3},{"l":"Jun 26","v":0.5},{"l":"Jul 26","v":4},{"l":"Aug 26","v":2.9},{"l":"Sep 26","v":2.3}],"latest":"+2.3%","name":"Retail Sales, y/y","next":"late Nov (Oct)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"+2.9%","read":"September rose 2.3% y/y against a 3.3% consensus (Trading Economics 3.8%) and fell 0.7% m/m after −1.2% in August; August was revised up to +2.9% from +2.7%. A seventh straight y/y gain, but the slowest since June and the second miss in a row. The household survey for September (10 Nov) was worse: spending fell 6.8% y/y against −3.1% and 2.8% m/m against +0.3%, the tenth straight annual fall and the steepest since September 2020, which the ministry blamed on rain every day of the month in Tokyo; the survey is small and volatile. With headline CPI at 2.1% in September, real retail growth is near zero and household spending is negative in real terms, consistent with confidence at 35.0.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Missed; household spending −6.8%","src":"METI via Trading Economics","trend":"down","trendWord":"Slowing","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/japan/retail-sales-annual"},{"change":"−0.1 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":2.6},{"l":"Nov 25","v":2.6},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.6},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2.7},{"l":"Feb 26","v":2.6},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2.7},{"l":"Apr 26","v":2.5},{"l":"May 26","v":2.5},{"l":"Jun 26","v":2.5},{"l":"Jul 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Aug 26","v":2.5},{"l":"Sep 26","v":2.4}],"latest":"2.4%","name":"Unemployment Rate","next":"late Nov to 1 Dec (Oct)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"2.5%","read":"September fell to 2.4% against a 2.5% consensus, matching July's 12-month low. Unemployed fell 100K to 1.67mn, employment rose 340K to 68.68mn, a four-month high, and the labour force rose 250K to 70.34mn. The range since 2022 is 2.4% to 2.8%, so the labour market is at the tight end of it.","signal":"good","signalWord":"Tight","src":"MIC via FX.co; history FRED","trend":"down","trendWord":"Back to the low","url":"https://www.fx.co/en/forex-news/3259063"},{"change":"+0.01","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":1.18},{"l":"Nov 25","v":1.18},{"l":"Dec 25","v":1.19},{"l":"Jan 26","v":1.18},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.19},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1.18},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.18},{"l":"May 26","v":1.17},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.18},{"l":"Jul 26","v":1.18},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1.18},{"l":"Sep 26","v":1.19}],"latest":"1.19","name":"Jobs-to-Applicants Ratio","next":"late Nov to 1 Dec (Oct)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"1.18","read":"Rose to 1.19 from 1.18 (consensus 1.18), the first increase since June and the highest since December, but still below the 1.2 level the framework treats as tight and down from 1.35 in late 2022. With unemployment at 2.4% and employment at a four-month high, labour demand firmed in September; the Tankan employment DI (−38 all firms) says shortages remain severe.","ref":1,"refLabel":"1.0 = one opening per applicant","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Below 1.2 line","src":"MHLW via FX.co","trend":"up","trendWord":"First rise since Jun","url":"https://www.fx.co/en/forex-news/3258824"},{"change":"+3.8 pt","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":6.1},{"l":"Dec 25","v":5.1},{"l":"Jan 26","v":16.8},{"l":"Feb 26","v":4.2},{"l":"Mar 26","v":11.7},{"l":"Apr 26","v":14.8},{"l":"May 26","v":17},{"l":"Jun 26","v":19.3},{"l":"Jul 26","v":23.2},{"l":"Aug 26","v":19.3},{"l":"Sep 26","v":16.7},{"l":"Oct 26","v":20.5}],"latest":"+20.5%","name":"Exports, y/y","next":"mid-Dec (Nov)","period":"Oct 2026","prior":"+16.7%","read":"October exports rose 20.5% y/y to ¥11,768.8bn, the highest monthly value on record and the 14th straight gain, against a +20.0% consensus and September's +16.7% (Trading Economics shows September revised to +16.5%). Export volume rose 5.8%, so the gain is not only price. Semiconductors +49.7% by value, electrical machinery +33.6%, machinery +21.2%, transport equipment +18.0% with motor vehicles +19.9%; by destination the US +23.9% (motor vehicles to the US +37.2%, surplus ¥690.6bn), Asia +22.0%, the EU +22.1%, China +13.3% (chip equipment to China +42.6%, motor vehicles to China −53.3%), the Middle East −15.0%. Imports rose 21.6% to ¥12.25tn against about +25.8% expected, on mineral fuels +36.5% and petroleum +60.5% by value for 0.7% less volume (crude from the US +738% by volume, from the Middle East −16.5%); the deficit was ¥476.5bn against ¥955bn expected, a sixth monthly deficit. External demand is the strongest leg of the economy and the chip chain is its whole engine; the US tariff deal is not showing in autos to the US (+37.2%). Next: November trade in mid-December.","signal":"good","signalWord":"Beat; chips +49.7%, volume +5.8%","src":"MoF trade statistics (provisional) via Xinhua, ActionForex, IndexBox and Trading Economics; Newsquawk (consensus); Zaikei (QUICK consensus)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Record, 14th gain","url":"https://english.news.cn/asiapacific/20261118/a9e3802d03274a27b6508b13f7aa7ff5/c.html"},{"change":"+0.6 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":-0.7},{"l":"Nov 25","v":-2.8},{"l":"Dec 25","v":-0.1},{"l":"Jan 26","v":1.4},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.9},{"l":"May 26","v":1.4},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.6},{"l":"Jul 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1.6},{"l":"Sep 26","v":2.2}],"latest":"+2.2%","name":"Real Wages, y/y","next":"7–8 Dec (Oct)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"+1.6%","read":"September real wages rose 2.2% y/y, a ninth straight gain and above the 1.3% consensus, after August was revised to +1.6% from +1.5%. Nominal cash earnings rose 4.6% to ¥311,409, the fastest since January 1997 and above the 3.8% consensus, an eighth month above 3%; August was revised to 3.9%. Base pay rose 3.7% (3.8% in August), same-sample full-time base pay 3.1% (2.9%) and overtime 3.6%; special payments jumped 32.6%, which the ministry attributes to a statistical quirk, so the headline overstates the trend. The deflator CPI was 2.4% (2.2%). The BoJ's virtuous-cycle test is met on base pay but not yet on spending: September household spending fell 6.8% y/y (10 Nov), so the income gain is being saved or lost to weather. The Summary of Opinions has one member expecting next year's wage growth to be consistent with 2% and another expecting little change from this year. October wages print 7–8 Dec.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = wages keep pace with prices","signal":"good","signalWord":"Positive 9 months","src":"MHLW via Reuters (Investing.com), ActionForex and Trading Economics","trend":"up","trendWord":"Faster growth","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy-news/japans-real-wages-rise-for-ninth-month-in-september-backing-boj-rate-hike-5006105"},{"change":"−1.5 pt","hist":[{"l":"Dec 23","v":1.9},{"l":"Mar 24","v":-0.4},{"l":"Jun 24","v":-0.5},{"l":"Sep 24","v":3.4},{"l":"Dec 24","v":1.6},{"l":"Mar 25","v":2},{"l":"Jun 25","v":0.5},{"l":"Sep 25","v":-1.5},{"l":"Dec 25","v":1.1},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.4},{"l":"Sep 26","v":-0.1}],"latest":"−0.1%","name":"Real GDP, q/q annualized","next":"8 Dec (Q3 2nd prelim)","period":"Q3 2026 (1st prelim)","prior":"+1.4%","read":"Real GDP fell 0.02% q/q and 0.1% annualized in July to September (first preliminary, 16 Nov), against a +0.1% q/q and about +0.3% annualized consensus (range −0.3% to +1.5%), the first contraction in four quarters; Q2 was revised to +0.4% q/q and +1.4% annualized. Private consumption fell 0.3% q/q for a second quarter (consensus −0.2%) on food and household goods, weather hits to clothing and lodging, and payback from pre-tax-change purchases of air conditioners and cars; housing −1.1%, government consumption −0.0% (first negative in six quarters), public investment −1.1%, exports −0.2% with R&D services down, imports +2.5% (crude re-sourced after Hormuz), so net exports took 0.6 pt while domestic demand added 0.6 pt. Business investment rose 1.6% q/q (+6.8% annualized, consensus +1.5%) on AI servers and chip equipment, the first rise in three quarters. Nominal GDP +0.4% q/q and +1.6% annualized; deflator +2.4% y/y. Analysts (Meiji Yasuda, Daiwa) read the consumption weakness as price-driven and no bar to a December hike; Mizuho's Katagi says the board split sharpens if it persists. The second estimate is 8 Dec, after the MoF corporate survey on capex.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = no growth","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Missed; consumption −0.3% q/q, capex +1.6%","src":"Cabinet Office ESRI first preliminary (16 Nov) via Admiral Markets, Seoul Economic Daily and Yahoo Finance; Mace News consensus","trend":"down","trendWord":"First fall in 4 quarters","url":"https://admiralmarkets.com/analytics/traders-blog/japan-gdp-q3-2026-yen-usdjpy-160"}],"sub":"Confirmation of what the leading set signalled.","title":"Coincident indicators"},{"id":"policy","rows":[{"change":"+0.1 pt","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":3},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.4},{"l":"Jan 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.5},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1.7},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.4},{"l":"May 26","v":1.4},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.6},{"l":"Jul 26","v":1.8},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1.7},{"l":"Sep 26","v":2.1},{"l":"Oct 26","v":2.2}],"latest":"2.2%","name":"Core CPI (ex fresh food), y/y","next":"18 Dec (Nov)","period":"Oct 2026","prior":"2.1%","read":"October core CPI rose 2.2% from 2.1%, under the 2.3% consensus (Reuters poll of 16; QUICK median 2.3%) and the highest since December 2025, a second month at or above the 2% target. Headline was 2.3% (September 2.1%); food inflation slowed to 2.2% from 2.9% while goods rose 3.2% (2.8%) and services 1.4%, so the mix is energy and goods rather than demand. With September real wages at +2.2% the reading is at target rather than squeezing consumption, and it leaves December intact: Minami (Norinchukin) expects a hike next month and another in March 2027 when inflation is likely to exceed 3%; Shinke (Dai-ichi Life) says only market instability could make the Bank stand pat. Tokyo CPI for November prints 27 Nov; national November CPI prints 18 Dec at 08:30, the morning of the BoJ decision.","ref":2,"refLabel":"2% = BoJ target","signal":"neutral","signalWord":"At target; missed 2.3%","src":"MIC via Reuters (Investing.com, AOL), Trading Economics, ChainCatcher (headline), Fisco via Zaikei (QUICK median)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Highest since Dec 2025","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/japan/core-inflation-rate"},{"change":"−0.2 pt","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":3},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.9},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2.5},{"l":"Feb 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.9},{"l":"May 26","v":1.8},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.7},{"l":"Jul 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Sep 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Oct 26","v":2.2}],"latest":"2.2%","name":"Core-core CPI (ex food and energy), y/y","next":"18 Dec (Nov)","period":"Oct 2026","prior":"2.4%","read":"Core-core CPI eased to 2.2% from September's 2.4%, a six-month high, so the demand-driven measure the BoJ watches is back at target. Goods prices rose 3.2% y/y (2.8% in September) and services 1.4% (1.5%), so the acceleration is in goods, food ex fresh slowed to 2.2% from 2.9%, and services inflation is still short of 2%. Tokyo core-core was 2.6% in October. The print trims the overshoot case Ueda made on 19 Nov (underlying inflation 'could run above 2%') without removing December; the pace argument for 2027 is what it bears on.","ref":2,"refLabel":"2%","signal":"neutral","signalWord":"At target, services 1.4%","src":"MIC via Reuters (Investing.com, AOL), Trading Economics","trend":"down","trendWord":"Eased from 6-month high","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/japan/core-inflation-rate"},{"change":"−0.1 pt","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":2.8},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.3},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.8},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1.7},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.5},{"l":"May 26","v":1.3},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.6},{"l":"Jul 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1.8},{"l":"Sep 26","v":2.7},{"l":"Oct 26","v":2.6}],"latest":"2.6%","name":"Tokyo Core CPI, y/y","next":"27 Nov (Nov; core consensus 2.9%)","period":"Oct 2026","prior":"2.7%","read":"October core eased to 2.6% from 2.7%, matching the 2.6% consensus, the first deceleration in five months, while headline rose to 2.8% (consensus 2.7%) on fresh food (+8.6%) and energy, which turned to +2.2% y/y from −1.9% as electricity (+1.7%) and city gas (+4.9%) lost subsidy support. Ex fresh food and energy slowed to 2.6% from 2.9% (consensus 2.7%); services 2.2% (2.3%), goods 3.5% (3.2%), food ex fresh 3.0% (3.6%), accommodation 6.7%. Core m/m +0.3% after +0.9%. Above target on every measure but the underlying pace cooled; national October CPI prints on 20 Nov (core consensus 2.1%).","ref":2,"refLabel":"2% = BoJ target","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Energy back, above 2%","src":"MIC via ActionForex, investingLive and Trading Economics","trend":"down","trendWord":"First dip in 5 months","url":"https://www.actionforex.com/live-comments/659199-tokyo-core-cpi-eases-to-2-6-but-energy-prices-revive-inflation-risks/"},{"change":"0 bp (8–1)","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":0.75},{"l":"Jan 26","v":0.75},{"l":"Feb 26","v":0.75},{"l":"Mar 26","v":0.75},{"l":"Apr 26","v":0.75},{"l":"May 26","v":0.75},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1},{"l":"Jul 26","v":1},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1},{"l":"Sep 26","v":1.25},{"l":"Oct 26","v":1.25},{"l":"24 Nov","v":1.25}],"latest":"1.25%","name":"BoJ Policy Rate","next":"18 Dec","period":"Held 30 Oct; 2-year 1.765% on 24 Nov","prior":"1.25%","read":"Held at 1.25% on 30 Oct by 8–1: Takata dissented for 1.50%. October core CPI 2.2% (20 Nov) is at target; October services PPI +4.2% y/y (25 Nov), the fastest since March 1990, with accommodation +12.1% and transport +9.4%, is the kind of services-price print the Bank cites for a hike. The market has nonetheless trimmed December: MogWatch OIS 78.2% at 01:21 JST on 25 Nov (84.7% on 20 Nov), implied 1.445% for 18 Dec, 1.495% for January and 1.609% for March, with 1.75% or higher by March at 46%; OANDA 78.7%; Minkabu about 81% on 22 Nov; the Reuters poll 86% of economists. The 2-year closed 1.765% on 24 Nov, the first close under 1.80% in nine sessions, and the ¥300bn 40-year auction on 25 Nov drew 2.63 cover, below the 12-month average. The board is split on the end-point: Tamura about 2%, Takata the middle of 1.1% to 2.5%, Koeda clearly above 1%, Asada and Sato limited room; Tamura speaks 26 Nov, Ueda 30 Nov. Under the framework the stance is Tightening with the pace now the question: a hike on 18 Dec to 1.50% is still the base case, and the equity drawdown and the weak super-long auction are the two things that could hold it.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Tightening; Dec pricing slips to about 79%","src":"Bank of Japan (schedule, speeches page, services PPI); MogWatch (OIS 25 Nov); BigGo Finance (OIS 20 Nov, board views); OANDA Japan FOMC preview (78.7%); Minkabu FX (about 81%); Reuters via Investing.com (poll); Trading Economics jp (2-year); Newsquawk (40-year auction)","trend":"flat","trendWord":"Hawkish hold","url":"https://www.boj.or.jp/en/about/press/koen_2026/data/ko261116a1.pdf"},{"change":"+¥1.2tn in Oct","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":697.9},{"l":"Dec 25","v":677.8},{"l":"Jan 26","v":682.9},{"l":"Feb 26","v":683.8},{"l":"Mar 26","v":663},{"l":"Apr 26","v":663.3},{"l":"May 26","v":664.4},{"l":"Jun 26","v":639.6},{"l":"Jul 26","v":644.3},{"l":"Aug 26","v":644.7},{"l":"Sep 26","v":625},{"l":"Oct 26","v":626.2}],"latest":"¥626tn","name":"BoJ Total Assets","next":"early Dec (Nov)","period":"Oct 2026","prior":"¥625tn","read":"Total assets were ¥626.2tn at 31 Oct (BoJ accounts, published 4 Nov), up ¥1.2tn from ¥625.0tn at 30 Sep, with JGB holdings ¥505.3tn (+¥1.3tn): purchases of about ¥2.3tn a month this quarter outran light October redemptions. The balance sheet is down from a ¥765tn peak in Aug 2024 as JGB purchases are cut by about ¥200bn a quarter, heading to ¥2tn a month from April 2027; the June plan targets JGB holdings of about ¥480tn by March 2027. Quarter-end months dip on redemptions (September −¥20tn), so the October uptick is the between-quarter pattern, not a change in policy; ETF sales of about ¥860bn a year start in January.","signal":"neutral","signalWord":"QT by taper","src":"Bank of Japan accounts (31 Oct and 30 Sep 2026); BoJ purchase plan","trend":"up","trendWord":"Flat after Q3 dip","url":"https://www.boj.or.jp/en/statistics/boj/other/acmai/release/2026/ac261031.htm"}],"sub":"Tracker rows added alongside the leading and coincident core. They are not part of the original indicator list.","title":"Inflation and policy"},{"id":"markets","rows":[{"change":"−6.5 bp","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.06},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2.24},{"l":"Feb 26","v":2.11},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2.345},{"l":"Apr 26","v":2.515},{"l":"May 26","v":2.65},{"l":"Jun 26","v":2.67},{"l":"Jul 26","v":2.79},{"l":"Aug 26","v":2.94},{"l":"Sep 26","v":3.063},{"l":"Oct 26","v":2.77},{"l":"24 Nov","v":2.72}],"latest":"2.720%","name":"10-Year JGB Yield","period":"24 Nov close","prior":"2.785% on 20 Nov","read":"The 10-year closed 24 Nov at 2.720% (Kyodo, the new 384th issue with a 3.1% coupon; Trading Economics 2.72%; Fisco 2.696% on an unstated basis), down 6.5 bp and the lowest in about two weeks, 2.695% intraday per Nikkei, as crude fell during the Tokyo day and the haven bid carried through the holiday even with the Nikkei up 3.91%; December futures closed 127.60 (+60 sen) in a 127.15 to 127.84 range. The 25 Nov 40-year auction (2.63 cover, 4.090%) was weak, and Trading Economics showed the 30-year at 3.96% and 40-year 4.14% afterwards with the 10-year little changed. The US 10-year closed 4.90% to 4.93% on 24 Nov, so the gap is about 220 bp. Under the framework the 10-year is in the middle of its November range (2.70% to 2.92%) and the fiscal signal has moved to the 30- and 40-year rows.","signal":"neutral","signalWord":"Haven bid","src":"Kyodo via OANDA Japan (2.720%, −6.5 bp); Fisco via Zaikei 878940 (futures range, 2.696%); Trading Economics (2.72%, curve); Nikkei headline (2.695% intraday); stock-marketdata.com (20 Nov row, 24 Nov 10-year); Newsquawk (40-year auction); Company Chronicle (US 10-year)","trend":"down","trendWord":"Lower","url":"https://www.oanda.jp/lab-education/market_news/kn_2026112401001059/"},{"change":"−5.5 bp","hist":[{"l":"5 Nov","v":1.855},{"l":"6 Nov","v":1.73},{"l":"9 Nov","v":1.744},{"l":"10 Nov","v":1.761},{"l":"11 Nov","v":1.855},{"l":"13 Nov","v":1.88},{"l":"16 Nov","v":1.87},{"l":"17 Nov","v":1.87},{"l":"18 Nov","v":1.86},{"l":"19 Nov","v":1.845},{"l":"20 Nov","v":1.82},{"l":"24 Nov","v":1.765}],"latest":"1.765%","name":"2-Year JGB Yield","period":"24 Nov close","prior":"1.820% on 20 Nov","read":"The 2-year closed 24 Nov at 1.765% (Trading Economics 1.7650, −5.1 bp on its basis; Fisco 1.766%), down 5.5 bp from the 20 Nov close of 1.820% and the first close under 1.80% in nine sessions, with the 5-year at 2.12% (Fisco 2.100%). Trading Economics showed 1.76% at midday on 25 Nov. December pricing slipped with it: MogWatch OIS 78.2% for 18 Dec (implied 1.445%) at 01:21 JST on 25 Nov against 84.7% on 20 Nov, the March implied rate 1.609% (1.634%); OANDA 78.7%; the Reuters poll still has 86% of economists for 1.50%. Under the framework a close under 1.80% says a hold is being priced in part; the October services PPI at +4.2% y/y and the Tokyo CPI on 27 Nov (core consensus 2.9%) are the next tests, with Tamura on 26 Nov and Ueda on 30 Nov the words.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Pricing slips","src":"Trading Economics jp (1.7650, 24 Nov); Fisco via Zaikei 878940 (1.766%, 5-year 2.100%); MogWatch (OIS probabilities 25 Nov); BigGo Finance (OIS 20 Nov); OANDA Japan FOMC preview (78.7%); Reuters via Investing.com (poll)","trend":"down","trendWord":"Lower","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/japan/2-year-note-yield"},{"change":"−3.5 bp","hist":[{"l":"5 Nov","v":3.865},{"l":"6 Nov","v":3.83},{"l":"9 Nov","v":3.85},{"l":"10 Nov","v":3.865},{"l":"11 Nov","v":3.94},{"l":"13 Nov","v":4},{"l":"16 Nov","v":4.03},{"l":"17 Nov","v":4.04},{"l":"18 Nov","v":4.045},{"l":"19 Nov","v":3.99},{"l":"20 Nov","v":3.975},{"l":"24 Nov","v":3.94}],"latest":"3.94%","name":"30-Year JGB Yield","period":"24 Nov close","prior":"3.975% on 20 Nov","read":"The 30-year closed 24 Nov at 3.94% (Trading Economics, −2.7 bp on its basis; no QUICK/NQN close was found), a third close under 4.00%, with the 20-year 3.63% (Fisco 3.605%) and the 40-year 4.12%, as the belly led (5-year −6 bp) and the super-long end lagged ahead of supply. The ¥300bn 40-year reopening on 25 Nov drew 2.63 cover against 3.10 in September and below the 12-month average (Bloomberg), with the highest accepted yield 4.090% (4.125% in September) at a price of 94.35; the lower yield says the concession was taken before the sale, the cover says real money did not chase it. Trading Economics showed the 30-year at 3.96% (+2 bp) and the 40-year at 4.14% (+2.5 bp) after the result. Under the framework rising super-long yields with the yen at 159.26 keep the warn pair on; a 30-year close above 4.05% while equities fall is the term-premium signal, 4.30% is the fiscal line, and a close under 3.90% with USD/JPY under 158.5 turns the pair off. The MoF meets primary dealers on 26 Nov and investors on 27 Nov with the 2027 issuance plan the subject.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Weak auction","src":"Trading Economics (30-year 3.94% on 24 Nov, 3.96% and 40-year 4.14% on 25 Nov); Fisco via Zaikei 878940 (20-year 3.605%); Newsquawk and StockNow (40-year auction: ¥300bn, 2.63 cover, 4.090%, 94.35); Bloomberg headline (below 12-month average); Media-IR (auction schedule, MoF meetings)","trend":"down","trendWord":"Lower","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/japan/30-year-bond-yield"},{"change":"+3.91%","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":50339.48},{"l":"Jan 26","v":53322.85},{"l":"Feb 26","v":58850.27},{"l":"Mar 26","v":51063.72},{"l":"Apr 26","v":59284.92},{"l":"May 26","v":66329.5},{"l":"Jun 26","v":70062.32},{"l":"Jul 26","v":64362.02},{"l":"Aug 26","v":66311.93},{"l":"Sep 26","v":66753.72},{"l":"Oct 26","v":63290.11},{"l":"24 Nov","v":58644.63}],"latest":"58,644.63","name":"Nikkei 225","period":"24 Nov close","prior":"56,436.65 on 20 Nov","read":"The Nikkei closed 24 Nov at 58,644.63 (+2,207.98, +3.91%), the first session after the holiday, with a 59,262.57 high (up more than 2,800 at one point per Jiji), Prime turnover about ¥8.07tn and 26 of 33 sectors up, led by nonferrous metals, electrical equipment and glass and ceramics; Advantest (+12.5%) and Tokyo Electron (+7.7%) were the largest contributors, Ibiden went limit-up, Murata and Taiyo Yuden rose sharply, Inpex fell a fourth session. The close was 2.3% under the 60,030 first-test line and 19.5% under the 72,831.73 June record. Wall Street then fell on 24 Nov (Nasdaq −1.48%, SOX −7.88%, Nvidia −5.86%) and Tokyo gave it back on 25 Nov: opened 58,383.49, morning close 56,710.43 (−1,934.20, −3.30%), futures 56,710 (−2,270), with Advantest (−841 points), Tokyo Electron (−593) and SoftBank Group (−409) about 95% of the fall while 866 Prime names rose against 640 and 21 of 33 sectors gained. Under the framework the rebound was the same AI names that led the fall and it did not hold; the 20 Nov liquidation close of 56,436.65 is 274 points under the morning level and a close below it is a new drawdown low. The Reuters poll (22 analysts, 16 to 24 Nov) has 65,000 by June 2027.","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Risk-off","src":"News On Japan (24 Nov close, high, turnover, sectors, stocks); ad-hoc-news/AP and Jiji (contributors, intraday); Reuters via WHTC (close, poll, record); Fisco via Zaikei 879072, OANDA Japan DZH mn_1045705 and Media-IR (25 Nov morning session); Motley Fool, Company Chronicle and TradingKey (24 Nov Wall Street)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Rebound","url":"https://newsonjapan.com/article/151515.php"},{"change":"+2.47%","hist":[{"l":"Dec 24","v":2784.92},{"l":"Mar 25","v":2658.73},{"l":"Jun 25","v":2852.84},{"l":"Sep 25","v":3137.6},{"l":"Dec 25","v":3408.97},{"l":"Mar 26","v":3497.86},{"l":"Jun 26","v":3994},{"l":"Sep 26","v":4108.65},{"l":"Oct 26","v":3965.07},{"l":"24 Nov","v":3764.88}],"latest":"3,764.88","name":"TOPIX","period":"24 Nov close","prior":"3,674.22 on 20 Nov","read":"TOPIX closed 24 Nov at 3,764.88 (+90.66, +2.47%), ending a five-session losing streak, with 26 of 33 sectors up; the gain was 1.4 points less than the Nikkei in percent because the rebound was concentrated in the AI complex. On 25 Nov it was 3,727.24 (−37.64, −1.00%) at the morning close against the Nikkei −3.30%: Prime 866 up, 640 down, 39 flat, and 21 of 33 sectors up (pharmaceuticals, food, other products) against nonferrous metals, electrical equipment and machinery down, so the broad market held while the three AI names fell 13% to 16%. The 24 Nov close was 3.3% under the 3,895 first-test line and 8.4% under the 4,108.65 September close; the 20 Nov low close is 3,674.22. Under the framework TOPIX holding while the Nikkei falls says the drawdown has narrowed to the AI leaders, which is a better breadth read than 20 Nov but not a stabilisation.","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Risk-off","src":"News On Japan (24 Nov close); OANDA Japan DZH mn_1045705 and Media-IR (25 Nov morning close, breadth, sectors); kabutore.biz quarterly closes","trend":"up","trendWord":"Rebound","url":"https://tbladvisory.com/20261120nk/"},{"change":"+0.24","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":156.4},{"l":"Jan 26","v":154.7},{"l":"Feb 26","v":155.9},{"l":"Mar 26","v":159.8},{"l":"Apr 26","v":160.2},{"l":"May 26","v":159.3},{"l":"Jun 26","v":161.9},{"l":"Jul 26","v":160.2},{"l":"Aug 26","v":160.1},{"l":"Sep 26","v":157.4},{"l":"Oct 26","v":157.55},{"l":"24 Nov","v":159.26}],"latest":"159.26","name":"USD/JPY","period":"24 Nov (New York close)","prior":"159.02 on 23 Nov","read":"USD/JPY closed 24 Nov in New York at 159.26 (OANDA Japan digest, +0.24; Fisco 159.35 at the close), with a 159.58 high in the Tokyo evening, above Monday's 159.50, and a 158.99 low near 22:00 JST when the WSJ report of new US tariffs pushed US futures and yields down; it recovered as crude rose and consumer confidence printed 103.3. Tokyo 24 Nov: 159.22 at 15:40 after a 158.75 morning low; 159.34 at 17:05. Tokyo 25 Nov: 159.31 to 159.34 at midday (Trading Economics) with the Nikkei down 3.3%, so the equity fall is not producing a yen bid this time. EUR/JPY 174.64 (+1.27, high 174.82), EUR/USD 1.0966 (+0.0063), dollar index low 103.75. The week's range is 158.42 to 159.97 and 160 has not traded; no MoF or Katayama comment dated 24 or 25 Nov was found, Trading Economics notes only \"increasingly firm verbal warnings\" on 23 Nov; the November intervention total prints 30 Nov. Under the framework the pair is in the intervention zone with the front-end gap about 290 bp (US 2-year about 4.65%, JGB 1.765%) and the carry-unwind path (155 to 156) now needs the equity fall to spread beyond the AI names.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Near 160","src":"OANDA Japan NY digest mn_1045662 (159.26 close, high, low, EUR/JPY, EUR/USD, dollar index, US 10-year, WTI, gold, US data); Fisco via Zaikei 878985 and Zai FX 499623 (159.35, ranges, oil); News On Japan (Tokyo 24 Nov levels); OANDA Japan FOMC preview (159.34 at 17:05, week range); Trading Economics (25 Nov midday)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Weaker yen","url":"https://www.oanda.jp/lab-education/market_news/mn_1045662_202611250720/"}],"sub":"Additions to the leading and coincident core. Closing levels for Wednesday 4 Nov unless a date is shown; 5 Nov figures are intraday.","title":"Market readings"}],"takeaways":["Leading indicators: no new print. The November flash PMIs stand: manufacturing 53.7 (October 53.3, consensus 53.5), output 53.8, export orders the second-fastest growth since January 2018; services 50.5 (51.7) with new business at a four-month low; composite 51.5, a six-month low; business confidence joint-lowest since August 2020. Standing: BoJ Tankan +24, Reuters Tankan +27 and +35, core machinery orders +13.4% y/y, consumer confidence 35.0, housing starts −2.7%. The Reuters poll of 22 analysts (16 to 24 Nov) has the Nikkei at 65,000 by June 2027 and 67,500 by end-2027, both under the 72,831.73 June record. Leading and coincident indices (September) print 26 Nov, consumer confidence 2 Dec.","Coincident data: no new print. October exports +20.5% y/y to a record ¥11,768.8bn; Q3 GDP −0.1% annualized (consumption −0.3% q/q, capex +1.6%; second estimate 8 Dec); industrial production +1.7% y/y; real wages +2.2% (nominal +4.6%); unemployment 2.4%; jobs ratio 1.19; retail sales +2.3%. The WSJ (24 Nov) says USTR may publish its Section 301 industrial-subsidy findings on 16 economies this week with Japan, Korea and the EU held to about 15% in total under their deals and autos, steel and aluminium excluded, and China higher; the export leg is unchanged until a rate is set. October industrial production, labour and retail print 27 to 30 Nov. With core CPI at 2.2% and real wages at +2.2% the virtuous-cycle test is still passed.","Prices and policy: October services PPI (BoJ, 25 Nov) +4.2% y/y from a revised 3.8%, the fastest since March 1990, m/m +1.1%; ex-international transport +3.2% (3.0%); international transport +99.1% (ocean freight +120.4%), transport and postal +9.4%, accommodation +12.1%, leasing +11.8%, real estate +3.0%, information and communications +2.5%. October national CPI core 2.2%, core-core 2.2%, headline 2.3%. December pricing: MogWatch OIS 78.2% at 01:21 JST on 25 Nov (84.7% on 20 Nov), implied 1.445%, March 1.609%; OANDA 78.7%; Minkabu about 81% (22 Nov); Reuters poll 86% of economists. JGBs closed 24 Nov at 2-year 1.765% (−5.5 bp), 5-year 2.12%, 10-year 2.720% (−6.5 bp, two-week low, 2.695% intraday), 20-year 3.63%, 30-year 3.94%, 40-year 4.12%, futures 127.60 (+60 sen). The 40-year reopening on 25 Nov: ¥300bn, cover 2.63 (September 3.10, below the 12-month average per Bloomberg), highest accepted yield 4.090% (4.125%), price 94.35; the 30-year was 3.96% and the 40-year 4.14% after it (Trading Economics). US: CME 95.8% for 25 bp on 8–9 Dec; US 10-year 4.90% to 4.93%; consumer confidence 103.3 (consensus 92.4). The front-end gap is about 290 bp, the 10-year gap about 220 bp. Tokyo CPI 27 Nov: consensus core 2.9%, headline 3.1%, core-core 2.7%.","Yen and oil: USD/JPY closed 24 Nov in New York at 159.26 (OANDA; +0.24 from 159.02; Fisco 159.35), with a 159.58 high in the Tokyo evening, above Monday's 159.50, and a 158.99 low near 22:00 JST when the WSJ tariff report pushed US futures and yields down; 159.34 at 17:05 JST on 24 Nov (OANDA), 159.31 to 159.34 at midday Tokyo on 25 Nov (Trading Economics). EUR/JPY 174.64 (+1.27) after a 174.82 high, EUR/USD 1.0966 (+0.0063) after 1.0980 on a stronger German Ifo, dollar index low 103.75 from a 104.53 high on 23 Nov. The week's range is 158.42 to 159.97 (OANDA) and 160 has not traded; no MoF or Katayama comment dated 24 or 25 Nov was found; the November intervention total prints 30 Nov. Oil: WTI January $82.05 (+1.88) after a Houthi attack on a Riyadh tower and continued Hormuz shipping attacks; gold $3,561.9 (−89.5).","Markets: Tokyo 24 Nov close: Nikkei 58,644.63 (+2,207.98, +3.91%), high 59,262.57, Prime turnover about ¥8.07tn, 26 of 33 sectors up (nonferrous metals, electrical equipment, glass and ceramics), Advantest +12.5% and Tokyo Electron +7.7% the largest contributors, Ibiden limit-up, Inpex down a fourth session; TOPIX 3,764.88 (+90.66, +2.47%), ending a five-session losing streak. Wall Street 24 Nov: S&P 500 7,201.09 (−36.08, −0.50%), Nasdaq 23,733.54 (−356.02, −1.48%), Nasdaq 100 −1.99%, Dow 50,018.70 (+423.39, +0.85%), Russell 2000 2,684.94 (+0.57%), VIX 26.36, SOX −7.88%; Nvidia $155.38 (−$9.67, −5.86%, 379m shares, Bernstein upgrade to $200), AMD $413.27 (−8.86%), Micron −10.97%, SanDisk −11.13%, HPE −16.31%, Broadcom $301.08 (−3.98%), Anthropic $81.91 (−6.5%, under the $83 IPO), Microsoft +0.7%, Apple +1.4%; Dell reported AI orders of $60.2bn with inventory nearly tripled. Tokyo 25 Nov morning: Nikkei 56,710.43 (−1,934.20, −3.30%), open 58,383.49, volume 1,268m shares, Prime turnover about ¥3.64tn, Nikkei 225 111 up and 110 down, Prime 866 up and 640 down, 21 of 33 sectors up; Advantest 23,375 (−3,900, −14.3%, −841 points), Tokyo Electron 7,773 (−1,173, −13.1%, −593), SoftBank Group 2,661 (−505, −16.0%, −409), Ibiden 4,485 (−887), Kioxia 6,820 (−1,214), Murata 4,533 (−867), Fujikura 2,080 (−338), Lasertec 29,215 (−3,985), Disco 40,220 (−4,430), Taiyo Yuden 5,874 (−1,000), KOKUSAI Electric 4,657 (−940); Fast Retailing 85,530 (+1,490, +121 points), Terumo +47, Konami +42, Chugai +42, Toyota 3,394 (+83); futures 56,710 (−2,270); TOPIX 3,727.24 (−37.64, −1.00%). The first stabilisation test (60,030, 3,895, VI under 35) was failed at the 24 Nov close by 2.3% and 3.3% and is 5.9% and 4.5% away at the morning levels."],"translation":[{"body":"The 24 Nov close (Nikkei 58,644.63, +3.91%; TOPIX 3,764.88, +2.47%) was the rebound the 20 Nov liquidation close invited, and it failed the first stabilisation test by 2.3% on the Nikkei and 3.3% on TOPIX. The 25 Nov morning (Nikkei −1,934.20 to 56,710.43, TOPIX −1.00%) then showed the shape of the market: three names (Advantest, Tokyo Electron, SoftBank Group) were about 95% of the fall while 866 Prime stocks rose and 21 of 33 sectors gained. Under the framework the index is a two-way market between the AI complex, which trades on the SOX (−7.88% on 24 Nov) and Nvidia, and a broad market that is holding; the Nikkei can make a new drawdown low under 56,436.65 on the first group alone. The posture stays Risk-off until 60,030 and 3,895 are retaken with the Nikkei VI under 35; the breadth on 25 Nov is the one better reading since 17 Nov.","title":"Equities"},{"body":"JGBs closed 24 Nov lower across the curve (2-year 1.765%, 10-year 2.720%, 30-year 3.94%, 40-year 4.12%, futures 127.60) with the belly leading, then the ¥300bn 40-year reopening on 25 Nov drew 2.63 cover against 3.10 in September and below the 12-month average at a 4.090% highest yield, after which the 30-year was 3.96% and the 40-year 4.14%. The read under the framework: the haven bid is in the 2- to 10-year sector, the super-long end is supply-driven, and the yen at 159.26 with the Nikkei down 3.3% on 25 Nov is not acting as a haven, so the warn pair (super-long yields with a weak yen) stays on. The 2-year under 1.80% and December pricing at about 78% say the market has moved part of the way to a hold without a BoJ word to justify it; the Tokyo CPI on 27 Nov (core consensus 2.9%) and Tamura on 26 Nov decide whether that reverses. USD/JPY sits 74 sen from 160 with the front-end gap about 290 bp and no MoF comment since 19 Nov.","title":"Fixed income and currency"},{"body":"The BoJ is tightening into an equity drawdown that is 22% from the record on the 25 Nov morning level, with inflation at target (core CPI 2.2%), services PPI at a 36-year high (+4.2%), real wages positive (+2.2%) and the flash composite PMI at a six-month low (51.5). The Fed is pricing 95.8% for 25 bp on 8–9 Dec with a cumulative 50 bp by January at 65.8%, so a BoJ hike to 1.50% leaves the gap unchanged; the OANDA note that the pace difference matters more than the December move is the right frame for the yen. The WSJ report of Section 301 tariffs on 16 economies with Japan near 15% in total and autos excluded is a fiscal and export watch item rather than a liquidity event. The fiscal side is the pressure point: a 40-year auction below its 12-month average, the MoF primary-dealer and investor meetings on 26 and 27 Nov on 2027 issuance, and the food consumption-tax cut (about ¥5tn a year, unfunded) in the Diet to 12 Dec. The balance sheet (¥626.2tn) is unchanged; the taper continues toward ¥2tn a month of JGB purchases from April 2027.","title":"Liquidity and policy"}]};
